o get this to happen it was it was  just like it would have taken him 10  years to finish his phd because he had  to learn so much more just to catch up  to that  um so again like in our group we have  this huge range of people this guy niall  who wrote this software all the way down  to kelby who searches yahoo to get to  his email  the other thing about scientists is that  they're all they're typically in a hurry  time is short and so writing a specific  code that's going to have a really nice  interface isn't going to be reusable is  hard and we don't like to do it it's  easy to just quickly code something up  use it for what we need and then yeah  maybe save it and come back to it later  but  to be honest you know if you come back  to your code that you wrote two months  ago you're not going to have any idea  what it did unless you actually  documented it well um  and so yeah so scripting in addition can  be a frustrating way to visualize data  so like we do have this great tool  matplotlib uh and pi plot and you might  do you know piplot.plot your your data  finally you have this big script and  then you plot it and it looks beautiful  oh but uh i think i want to change the  color of those dots or the shape or i  want to  change the the axes or i want to change  the title you have to go back and rerun  your entire script uh to just to change  that one little thing and you might have  to iterate over that several times until  you get what you finally want so it's  just a it's a frustrating way to  approach a visual problem  with a scripting backend  so avoplot aims to sort of address these  questions in a few different ways  the first of which the first very  obvious thing is that it's essentially a  gui front end to matplotlib so it has  all the functionality of matplotlib  you can import  various types of data using text csv  files you can mainly manually type it in  and then you see your your plot in this  wonderful map window and you can change  the colors and the fonts and the styles  and everything so it just makes that  step easier  and in addition it has basic data  processing capabilities and this is  something that's definitely extensible  and we're trying to add tons more  features on this this end of things  but for example fitting a line is to  your data is one thing we can do we're  fitting a gaussian curve to something  things like that  and you can do that right within the  graphical interface which is nice  um  again like like i said you know moving  things around or changing your your plot  is just made easier in a drag and drop  interface i mean we love scripting of  course but sometimes you just want to  point and click  in addition apple plot is very easily  customizable so i'm going to talk in a  bit about plug-ins which is a huge part  of avo plot  and probably my favorite thing about it  basically it's a framework for  scientists to create their own  processing tools so someone even with  minimal  computer skills minimal coding skills  can create essentially their own  software program that has a gui and runs  with python in the background  so instead of very powerful tools  so here's basically the anatomy of of  our program and uh the three sort of  main points that we like to that we like  to think that we try to accomplish so  like i said the first thing is the gui  interface fantastic  um the second thing is plugins  like so like i was just mentioning  you can write a plugin for your specific  data type to import specific data from  whatever kind of file you have you can  work with it you can do all of the  processing either behind the scenes or  at the click of a button  and the third thing is i'm going to talk  about as well is our scripting interface  which is very new  but we hope to expand upon this  so  if you like to work in  mostly in a script interface rather than  a gui that's fine too and we think that  this  feature is going to be very good for  the more advanced programmers so you c