d we all come from different  backgrounds both scientifically speaking  and  i.t speaking so  some people in our group have zero  computer programming skills and some  people  have very high computer programming  skills  personally i don't have a very strong  computer science background i will tell  you that right now and i will remind you  that throughout the talk that actually  this guy actually wrote 99.9 of the  program i'm going to talk to you about  today so of course if you have any  problems with it you can contact the  developer  um  about two years ago i started coding  python because of this guy nyle peters  before that my coding experience was  basically html and visual basic  so he taught me how to work with data in  a language like python which is very  good for doing math and science  but now i've been able to to help him a  little bit like i said the point one  percent of the code that wasn't by him  was by me um so i've grown a lot in that  two years  and uh i think that the fact that i'm  able to give this talk to you today sort  of embodies the idea of avo plot that is  someone who's not  an awesome computer programmer someone  who just has basic skills but knows what  they want to do and has a problem to  achieve and some data to work with  can essentially make their own computer  program that has a gui and is powered by  python and uses the very excellent  libraries that python already has to  offer  so yeah i guess the point is sort of if  i can do it anyone can that's my  message i also wanted to briefly mention  the software sustainability institute  who has a presence here today um so  there's two people sitting in the back  that if you have any questions about the  ssi you should speak with them  and i recommend that you check them out  they uh sponsored me in 2013 as a fellow  gave me an allowance of 3000 pounds to  travel to conferences and go to  workshops and things like that  and they can be a fantastic tool for  people who want to either publish  scientific  programs or  learn how to use different programs and  different tools and organize workshops  and things like that so i would  encourage you to do that if you're  interested  okay so now back to the story of apple  plot  so as i said my uh my team is a group of  volcanologists and and the avo plot  program really grew grew from the need  to solve real world problems so we like  to think that that's where we're coming  from is having real world problems and  and giving you an easy solution to work  with them  so avocad started in field work in chile  a couple years ago and this is  a picture of an erupting volcano called  pujawe cordon caye in southern chile  you probably haven't heard of it that's  because it's in the southern hemisphere  and no one really noticed up here but  it's kind of the equivalent of the  southern hemisphere equivalent of the  iceland eruption  so the ash from from this volcano made  its way into the stratosphere and  actually circumnavigated the entire  globe so that means it traveled all the  way around to the other side of the  earth canceled flights in new zealand  and then kept going and ended up back in  chile where it started so this was a  huge event  and while we were there we brought all  kinds of equipment so  here's some of the the devices that we  use which are  some everything from standard video  cameras  to spectrometers that look at the the  gases and the ash coming out of the  volcanic plume  in an attempt to measure how much stuff  is coming out of the volcano  so again we have all these different  it's called multi-parameter monitoring  where you have all these different tools  looking at the same thing at the same  time  and we had a  multitude of tools to help us do that  that were mostly written by postdocs and  phd students and staff years ago so we  had all this legacy code that didn't  work well together and to be honest none  of it really worked well at all  for example many of the of the tools we  were using you have to physically edit  the scripts in a text editor in the  field to do different parts of the  program that you want to run the  equipment  which is not easy in the field or with  gloves on and it's also not easy for  those of our group who had not done any  computer programming before so there was  a huge barrier for them  in addition there's there's a big need  for real-time data visualization so we  have data streaming into our computers  we want to be able to look at it right  away and see in the field real time and  maybe even do some small bits of  processing on that data to see what  we're actually looking at  and that can be very important on field  work when  very often as scientists our field work  is sort of a once-in-a-lifetime  opportunity right so we're in the field  and we need to capture what's happening  in that moment  and for example  with the uh with the old scripts that we  used to use uh more often than not our  spectrometers would be pointed somewhere  not in the plume at all it would be  pointed out in space and so we would end  up with data that was nothing at all we  would spend an entire day in the field  and come back and realize oops we  actually didn't measure anything so  being able to look at your data as it's  coming in is really important  the other place where hablo plot was  heavily developed it was in a rather  chilly garage in antarctica so this is  uh  the lower erebus hut which is a  small science station about the size of  a garage on the side of an active  volcano in antarctica and this is where  much of the programming was done by that  guy with the big arrow nile  um  and i'll quote him here by saying  if you think that the code is messy then  think about how hard it is to type with  gloves on  so this is  so at arabis uh volcano uh we have a  large team there about  12 people are are at the base every  every year and everyone is doing  completely different things so  um  even more so than at that big volcano in  chile where we have people uh using  infrared cameras to look at the active  lava lake that's in the volcanic crater  we have people looking at the gas plume  uh we have people taking lidar data of  the ice caves that are on the ice around  the volcano we have all kinds of  different  studies happening there um so this is  where a lot of our custom tools  have happened and were developed it's  because we had all these needs that  arose and as a need for some new data  processing arose we thought hey we can  we can add this to avo plot we'll use  this this plotting program we have  which i'll eventually show you i promise  and we can add add this feature to work  with this specific data set  so the features have have largely been a  result of a direct need  um so avo plot started its life as a  tool for for visualizing gas data so the  stuff that we were doing here but  eventually um we found that we were  reusing bits of code from here and there  and that we were able to make more and  more tools and add on more and more  features  all right so so here we are with all  this data and bits of python code kind  of scattered about  and trying to work through it all  but eventually this would all build into  something greater which is avail plot  and this is our logo here  very nice avocado so that was basically  the brief history of the beginnings of  this project so a bunch of  volcanologists running around the world  at various volcanoes collecting data and  kind of trying to figure out what to do  with it and realizing that maybe it  could be better and then realizing maybe  other people could use this tool as well  so  next i'm going to talk a bit about  sort of the the more broad data handling  problems faced by scientists in general  um and i suppose it applies to  non-scientists as well handling data is  is a thing across many disciplines  and our examples come from personal  experience but like i said they're  widely applicable  and then we'll kind of combine that with  a look at what python tools are already  available and what we can do with those  tools  and then finally we'll talk about the  apple plot software itself uh what it  aims to achieve where we are in the  current design  and what we'd like to see in the future  and ideas for implementing new features  so  by its nature  science is data driven  um as such most of chemistry physics  biology geology  can be broken down into into a few key  steps  so firstly you collect a bunch of data  somewhere  often many different types of data for a  single project  and this this in particular is the  highly specialized step  so your data is always going to be very  specific and you might think that i i  need a really specialized tool to work  with this because  it's not easy as easy as just dumping it  in a spreadsheet  but then of course you import your data  into some kind of analysis software and  this  these are generally very these are often  very general tools like spreadsheets or  plain text data files csv files um but  again can be quite specialized like a  binary spectrum file for example  so then you want to do some processing  on your data  and as with the data import this can be  very general  um like fitting a line or a curve to  your data or again something very  specialized like fitting backgrounds or  modifying your your curve in some way  and finally you might iterate between  the processing and visualization steps  enough times until you get a product  that that looks the way you want it to  look either for publication or for  looking at the data in a visual way to  try to figure out what's going on  so okay so what's the problem then  so we need a general tool that can be  used for  specialized  data sets  that's pretty self-explanatory but the  second point i think is a bit is a bit  more subtle um  so there's no there's no point in having  to to repeat the work of specialization  uh each time you have to to process new  data  um  and there already may be tools used to  accomplish this so spreadsheets can be  used as templates to make  specializations reusable  and of course like i said we have a ton  of python tools to do this right  so you might say well python solves this  problem already so  so why are we even looking at this i  mean it's a general tool it can deal  with highly specialized cases  you can import general data like csv  using csv module  specialized cases can be handled using  custom written code for your particular  data set  and the same can be said for the  processing and visualization steps there  already exists these tools um  to to to work with the data and there's  of course many many more this is an  example of some of the modules that that  we use in our group  okay so if python already answers our  question then what's the real problem  here that we want to address um as i  mentioned before a lot of scientists are  not programmers um so although some will  happily write a script to achieve their  task others might find that completely  you know unapproachable um  so i'll give you one quick example  a member of our group named kelby who's  a brilliant guy  but not so brilliant when it comes to  i.t stuff  for example i once literally witnessed  him open a browser  and in the uh the search box which is a  google search he typed in yahoo  and then clicked the first result on  google which was yahoo.com and then in  the yahoo search box he typed yahoo mail  and then he clicked the first link and  that's how he got to his mail that's how  he got to his email was by  and i was just like watching horror as  this was happening  and this is a guy who was using all the  tools that we were in the field and  trying to work with these scripts and it  was just not something he had done  before and so there's a huge learning  curve for him right like he knew how to  how to work with the data on a more  uh general level but when it came to  okay how do i you know what what do i  type to get this to happen it was it was  just like it would have taken him 10  years to finish his phd because he had  to learn so much more just to catch up  to that  um so again like in our group we have  this huge range of people this guy niall  who wrote this software all the way down  to kelby who searches yahoo to get to  his email  the other thing about scientists is that  they're all they're typically in a hurry  time is short and so writing a specific  code that's going to have a really nice  interface isn't going to be reusable is  hard and we don't like to do it it's  easy to just quickly code something up  use it for what we need and then yeah  maybe save it and come back to it later  but  to be honest you know if you come back  to your code that you wrote two months  ago you're not going to have any idea  what it did unless you actually  documented it well um  and so yeah so scripting in addition can  be a frustrating way to visualize data  so like we do have this great tool  matplotlib uh and pi plot and you might  do you know piplot.plot your your data  finally you have this big script and  then you plot it and it looks beautiful  oh but uh i think i want to change the  color of those dots or the shape or i  want to  change the the axes or i want to change  the title you have to go back and rerun  your entire script uh to just to change  that one little thing and you might have  to iterate over that several times until  you get what you finally want so it's  just a it's a frustrating way to  approach a visual problem  with a scripting backend  so avoplot aims to sort of address these  questions in a few different ways  the first of which the first very  obvious thing is that it's essentially a  gui front end to matplotlib so it has  all the functionality of matplotlib  you can import  various types of data using text csv  files you can mainly manually type it in  and then you see your your plot in this  wonderful map window and you can change  the colors and the fonts and the styles  and everything so it just makes that  step easier  and in addition it has basic data  processing capabilities and this is  something that's definitely extensible  and we're trying to add tons more  features on this this end of things  but for example fitting a line is to  your data is one thing we can do we're  fitting a gaussian curve to something  things like that  and you can do that right within the  graphical interface which is nice  um  again like like i said you know moving  things around or changing your your plot  is just made easier in a drag and drop  interface i mean we love scripting of  course but sometimes you just want to  point and click  in addition apple plot is very easily  customizable so i'm going to talk in a  bit about plug-ins which is a huge part  of avo plot  and probably my favorite thing about it  basically it's a framework for  scientists to create their own  processing tools so someone even with  minimal  computer skills minimal coding skills  can create essentially their own  software program that has a gui and runs  with python in the background  so instead of very powerful tools  so here's basically the anatomy of of  our program and uh the three sort of  main points that we like to that we like  to think that we try to accomplish so  like i said the first thing is the gui  interface fantastic  um the second thing is plugins  like so like i was just mentioning  you can write a plugin for your specific  data type to import specific data from  whatever kind of file you have you can  work with it you can do all of the  processing either behind the scenes or  at the click of a button  and the third thing is i'm going to talk  about as well is our scripting interface  which is very new  but we hope to expand upon this  so  if you like to work in  mostly in a script interface rather than  a gui that's fine too and we think that  this  feature is going to be very good for  the more advanced programmers so you can  write a program instead of calling uh  piplot.plot you might call avap.plot and  then instead of opening your graph in a  matplotlib window it will open it in  apple plot so you can do your whole  script run everything just like you  normally would and just import avo plot  as a module  open up avo plot and then ruin your axes  and make your your graph look nice look  the way you want it to look  okay so i showed you i felt bad for  putting up so many slides filled with  text  so i wanted to have a quick uh story to  get your attention back  so so why is it called apple plot well  it's so named based on the ramblings of  someone who would actually eventually  become one of his end users  a guy called calvin barney who's another  member of our research group uh  he he's a big fan of things like  mathematica  which not to say mathematica isn't great  but he's a fan of this on the basis that  if it costs money it's better  and he decided to go on to a big rant on  a very hot day in the middle of the  ethiopian desert a few years ago and  explained to nyle the guy who wrote this  program why open source is bad and why  paying for programs is good  and so he was he this is paraphrasing  but to the best of our memory he said  something like  open source software is written by a  bunch of hippies living in a commune in  palo alto with their beards down to  their socks and sandals living on  nothing but organic avocados oh look at  me i'm so moral  so  in uh recognition of telvin's epic rant  on how much he hated open source  software and hated using linux and  hitting having to check the man pages  and write a wrapper for his usb driver  uh we named it after after that apple  plot  and like i said before this guy is now  actually using ammo plot so and he's  scripting in python now as well so we've  converted him i think  okay so finally a screenshot so this is  what apple plot looks like this is the  gui and i'll kind of take you through  take you through the screenshot a bit to  show you sort of the different sections  so we've broken it down into panels and  also tabs as well so you can open as  many panels as you like  well as many panels until the program  crashes but you know  in addition as many tabs as you like as  well  so you can look at for example  this is some data that that we look at  at volcanoes and we're looking at the  gases coming out of volcanoes and these  are the kind of data that are good to  see in real time  so this is a this is just running a  script that will pull data from a from a  folder will continually watch the folder  for new new files whenever a new file is  written it'll update these graphs and  then we can watch what's happening in  the volcano and be sure that our  instrument is actually pointed where we  want it to be pointed  um  so the current the current phase of  development is that it's very early  um but we have a stable release which is  available on google code um  and for the stable release we focus on  implementing a few number of fully  workable features rather than a bunch of  half-baked ideas although we have many  of those in the works too  for the back end we have ditched the map  outlet back-end and we actually use wx  so you do have to have that installed  and  okay so yeah  um  so when you're writing when you're  writing uh if you're writing a plugin  for example for for avo plot  you you work in like i said you have  these panels and um  so the  the navigation panel you can select  either which which window you're looking  at or which tab or which you can plot  multiple lines for example in a single  graph so you could select which line  and then the control panel will change  depending on which thing you've selected  in the navigation panel so if you've  selected a particular line you might  have tools to  to  our particular data set you might have  tools to fit a curve or to change the  color of the line  or to perform  math and processing on that data  if you've selected the the whole subplot  then you'll have these control panel  tools where you can change like the the  axes and the title and the um the color  of the plot window and things like that  oh and notice that all of the matplotlib  tools are readily available so  like the ones you know and love the zoom  and pan and home and all that are just  right there on the top  okay and then something i mentioned  before that we're really excited about  is this brand new scripting interface  which we just released with the the  latest release  um  and  so as well as this basic gui um  you can you can work with avoplot uh  from a scripting interface um and  although only a few commands are  currently um  implemented but we hope to to grow it  and also one thing we're hoping to to  achieve here is maybe get get more of  you developing on it as well which would  be fantastic  so it's basically designed to mimic  the pi plot interface um making it  really simple for people who already use  matplotlib to just like i said import  avo plot and plot and then it opens your  plot in in our program instead  um  and so that if you have existing scripts  for example they can already be changed  very easily  uh plugins which i think is is what  makes this really powerful um and very  very usable for people who maybe aren't  as avid as coders but maybe just  starting out  is uh is is the fact that it's a plug-in  interface  so the most simple the most simple  plug-in  like just a few lines of code um  will allow unsupported data files uh to  be loaded like  by a point and click of like file new  um but more complex ones allow you to  find define your own access specific  data series types  specific data processing tools you know  adding buttons and things like that  plugins are distributed using disk g  tills  and a bunch of so so loads of loads of  scripts like quick and dirty scripts are  written by scientists every day right  but we'd like to offer avo plot as sort  of a framework for them t